# GUI panels and lore

When adding or changing CivCraft GUIs (HexaCore `Window`, `Panel`, `Button`), keep tooltip text on the type that owns the slot and prefer **reusable panel classes** over one-off list wiring in every window.

See also: `gui-button-set-functionality.mdc` (click hints via `setFunctionality`, not duplicate lore).

## Lore: build it on the owning GUI type

Put `Icon.addLore` / `String.format` lore in the **window**, **panel subclass**, or a **private** `fillButtons` / `buildXButton` method on that same class — not a new `*Display`, `*Lore`, or `*LoreBuilder` type for one screen.

Shared lore for a reusable region (e.g. town daily quest slots) belongs on that **panel** (`TownDailyQuestPanel.addDailyQuestBodyLore`), not a separate display class.

```java
// ❌ BAD — extra class only to format lore for one window
GuideQuestLoreHelper.addObjectiveLore(icon, step);

// ✅ GOOD — lore lives where the button is built (same pattern as CivProjectWindow)
private Button buildObjectiveButton(QuestStepConfig step) {
    Icon icon = Icon.buildBase(material, String.format("%s%s", CivColor.Gold, title));
    icon.addLore(String.format("%s%s", CivColor.Gray, step.description));
    icon.addLore("");
    icon.addLore(String.format("%sProgress: %s%s/%s", CivColor.Gold, CivColor.White, current, total));
    Button button = new Button(icon);
    button.setFunctionality(click -> this.openChoice(), "choose your path", ClickType.LEFT, ClickType.RIGHT);
    return button;
}
```

Follow `java-civcolor-formatting` and `java-string-format-messages` for colors and dynamic text.

## Prefer `LoreBlock` over `QuestLoreLines` and manual lore loops

Do **not** add YAML wrapper types such as `QuestLoreLines extends ArrayList<String>`. Store plain lines on config beans and build HexaCore `LoreBlock` at the GUI boundary.

| Layer | Prefer | Avoid |
|-------|--------|-------|
| YAML / config POJO | `List<String>` with `setLore(Object)` → `WordUtil.normalizeLines` | `QuestLoreLines`, other `ArrayList` subclasses for scalar/sequence YAML |
| Icon tooltip | `icon.addLore(new LoreBlock(LoreType.LORE, lines))` | Per-line `icon.addLore(String.format("%s%s", CivColor.Gray, line))` or `QuestLoreDisplay.addDescriptionLore` for simple config text |
| Chat / NPC / legacy export | `WordUtil.joinForChat(lines)` on the raw `List<String>` | Storing display-colored `LoreBlock` on config types |

`LoreBlock` implements `Iterable<String>`; `Icon.addLore(Iterable)` uses `getSortedLore()`, which applies the block's `LoreType` default color. Use `LoreType.LORE` for plain gray YAML description lines; use `LoreType.DESCRIPTION` when the block is part of a structured title + body layout (same as `ShowBuildWindow`).

```java
// ❌ BAD — custom YAML list type + manual gray lore helper
private QuestLoreLines lore;
QuestLoreDisplay.addDescriptionLore(icon, track.getLore());

// ✅ GOOD — normalize YAML once on the config bean; LoreBlock at the icon
private List<String> lore;

public void setLore(Object lore) {
    this.lore = WordUtil.normalizeLines(lore);
}

public List<String> getLore() {
    return this.lore;
}

if (WordUtil.hasContent(track.getLore())) {
    icon.addLore(new LoreBlock(LoreType.LORE, track.getLore()));
}
```

For mixed sections (summary, rewards, progress), compose one or more `LoreBlock`s with `addSubBlock` on the window/panel — same pattern as `ShowBuildWindow` and `BuildingWonderWikiWindow` — instead of a quest-specific lore display helper.

### When a separate helper is OK

- The same lore block is used in **3+ unrelated** windows or commands — colocate on the **panel** that owns those slots (`TownDailyQuestPanel`) or the most natural existing owner, not a new `*Display` / `*LoreBuilder` class.
- **Do not add** new static methods to legacy `*Display` types (`QuestLoreDisplay`, …). Put new GUI lore on the window or panel you are changing.

### Legacy `*Display` classes

Do not add new `*Display` / `*LoreBuilder` types. When touching call sites of legacy helpers (`QuestLoreDisplay`, …), prefer moving lore onto the owning window or panel (`TownDailyQuestPanel`) over expanding the display class.

## Layout: reusable `Panel` subclasses over ad-hoc lists

Prefer a **generic `Panel` subclass** (HexaCore `gui.container.Panel`) that encapsulates dimension, fill, and list behavior. Reuse that panel wherever the same strip/grid is needed so layout and glass fill stay consistent.

```java
// ❌ BAD — bespoke LayoutListView + Panel wiring copy-pasted per window
LayoutListView<Quest> list = new LayoutListView<>(new BasicLayout(new Dimension(7, 1)), this::fillButtons);
Panel p = new Panel(this, new Dimension(7, 1));
p.addUIElement(list, Anchors.TOP_LEFT);
p.setFill(FILLING);
this.addUIElement(p, Coordinate.ONE_ONE);

// ✅ GOOD — one panel type, reused (see TemplatePanel, DepositPanel, GuideQuestObjectiveRow)
TemplatePanel templates = new TemplatePanel(this, resident)
        .setOwnedAction(this::applyTemplate, "apply template");
this.addUIElement(templates, Anchors.TOP_LEFT);
```

Inside a reusable panel, a single `LayoutListView` (or `ListView`) is fine — the panel **is** the abstraction, not every window inventing its own list + layout pair.

### Prefer panels when

- A fixed-size region (toolbar, template grid, deposit area, objective column) appears in more than one place or may later.
- You want the same `FILLING`, dimensions, and anchoring everywhere.

### `LayoutListView` directly on the window is OK when

- The list is **unique** to that window and unlikely to be reused — still build buttons with lore on the window (private methods), not a new display class.

## Checklist for new GUIs

1. Lore on the window/panel method that creates the `Button` / `Icon`.
2. Click instructions via `setFunctionality` — not manual "Click to…" lore (`gui-button-set-functionality.mdc`).
3. Reusable regions → `extends Panel` under `ui/gui/panel/` or next to the feature window; avoid N slightly different `LayoutListView` setups.
